# /etc/systemd/system/gripperx-bringup.service
[Unit]
Description=GripperX ROS2 Bringup (arm + servos + drive)
After=local-fs.target network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# PROPOSAL (OP-20 E / HWR-40), activate together with gripperx.target: the
# micro-ROS agent carries the ESP32 link, and ros2_control cannot read
# /hw/joint_states without it. Ordering it before bringup also reverses
# correctly on stop -- the agent goes down LAST, so the link is still alive
# during the >=1 s window in which the ESP32's CMD_TIMEOUT_MS zeroes the
# motors (see the TimeoutStopSec rationale below). Wants= (not Requires=), so
# a dead agent does not take the whole bringup with it.
#After=gripperx-agent.service
#Wants=gripperx-agent.service
# PROPOSAL: makes "systemctl stop gripperx.target" reach this unit.
#PartOf=gripperx.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ubuntu
Group=ubuntu
Environment=HOME=/home/ubuntu
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/gripperx-bringup.sh

# --- STOP CONTRACT (SR-12 / OP-20 option E) ---------------------------------
# Until 2026-08-19 this unit had no KillMode, no KillSignal, no ExecStop and no
# TimeoutStopSec, i.e. it inherited "SIGTERM to the whole cgroup, SIGKILL after
# 90 s". Both halves of that are wrong for a ros2 launch tree:
#
# 1) SIGTERM is the ONE signal `ros2 launch` handles badly. Its own handler
#    (launch/launch_service.py, _on_sigterm) logs "using SIGTERM can result in
#    orphaned processes / make sure no processes launched are still running"
#    and merely cancels its asyncio task -- it does NOT terminate its children.
#    Measured on the machine 2026-08-18 (journal 40c87ce0): the launch parent
#    was gone after 2 s and left arm_action_server, steer_servo_node,
#    teleop_mux_node and ros2_control_node running as real orphans. On SIGINT
#    launch instead runs its documented ordered teardown (SIGINT to each child,
#    escalating to SIGTERM after sigterm_timeout=5 s and to SIGKILL after a
#    further sigkill_timeout=5 s). KillSignal=SIGINT is therefore a correctness
#    fix, not a preference: it is the only signal the whole ROS 2 stack -- launch,
#    rclpy and rclcpp alike -- treats as "shut down cleanly".
#
# 2) KillMode: control-group is kept DELIBERATELY (it is also the default, but
#    it is written out here so it cannot be lost by accident). It sends the
#    KillSignal to EVERY process in the cgroup, so each node runs its own
#    destroy_node() teardown -- steer_servo_node disabling servo torque,
#    lidar_power_node driving LiDAR power ON before releasing the GPIO line --
#    even if the launch parent is wedged. That case is not hypothetical: on
#    2026-08-17 the bringup parent (PID 16300) ignored SIGINT and kept running.
#    KillMode=mixed would signal only the launch parent and would give us a
#    cross-node ORDER, but it makes the whole teardown depend on the very
#    process that has already been observed to ignore the signal, and a
#    TimeoutStopSec expiry would then SIGKILL everything with no torque-off at
#    all. Revisit mixed only once the launch-side fix (OP-20 option D) is in and
#    the SIGINT path is proven on hardware.
#    NOTE ON Q-SD3: the SR-12 rev-3 / OP-20 draft recommends option E1 =
#    `ExecStop` + KillMode=mixed, with KillSignal left at its SIGTERM default.
#    That is the right END STATE and this file is written to grow into it (see
#    the ExecStop block below for why it cannot be filled in yet). Until the
#    ExecStop script exists, `mixed` would be strictly worse -- it signals only
#    the launch parent, which has been observed to ignore the signal, and with no
#    script to empty the cgroup the nodes would then be SIGKILLed with no
#    teardown at all. So: control-group + SIGINT NOW, mixed + ExecStop LATER, and
#    the switch happens in one step together with the script. The SIGINT-vs-
#    SIGTERM divergence from the draft is a deliberate INPUT to Q-SD3, not a
#    decision taken here -- see the report.
KillMode=control-group
KillSignal=SIGINT

# TimeoutStopSec: PROVISIONAL 20 s, TO-VERIFY BY MEASUREMENT -- this is the one
# number in this file the user has to decide (OP-20: "concrete KillMode/
# TimeoutStopSec values are TO-VERIFY and must be set together with HWR-40's
# ordered sequence"). What it has to be longer than, worst case, added up:
#   * 10 s -- `ros2 launch`'s own internal escalation for a child that does not
#     exit: sigterm_timeout 5 s (SIGINT -> SIGTERM) + sigkill_timeout 5 s
#     (SIGTERM -> SIGKILL), both launch defaults, both parallel across children.
#   * >=1 s -- the ESP32's CMD_TIMEOUT_MS 1000 window (main.cpp L113). The
#     firmware timeout is on RECEIVING a message, so the wheels are only zeroed
#     by stopMotors() one full second after the last /hw/joint_commands.
#     SIGKILLing the stack before that second has elapsed means the drive is
#     never commanded to zero at all and only coasts to a stop. This is the
#     BACKSTOP and HWR-40 criterion 6 forbids it as the mechanism -- but while
#     D-SD1 blocks the commanded stop (see ExecStop below) it is in fact what
#     stops the drive, so the window has to fit inside the budget.
#   * the slowest serial teardown -- steer_servo_node turns torque off on 4
#     servos, arm_action_server on 6, each a bus round trip that RETRIES on a
#     non-responding servo (the arm is currently logging "servo 1 not
#     responding", so it is the slow one).
# NOT in this budget, and this is the update of 2026-08-19: there is NO ESP32
# teardown wait. Q-SD2 is resolved as Option 0 -- no new firmware interface; the
# agent is simply stopped and the ESP's own ping timeout fires destroyEntities()
# (main.cpp L179), which calls stopMotors() FIRST and then sets the session
# destroy timeout to 0, i.e. it never waits for an agent acknowledgement. The
# teardown is local, fast and autonomous, so the halt path has nothing to wait
# for. TimeoutStopSec is therefore decoupled from Q-SD1 and can be decided on
# its own -- which is what had blocked this number until now.
# 10 + 1 + margin -> 20 s. Anything below ~12 s truncates launch's own
# escalation; the old inherited 90 s is not dangerous, just far too slow for an
# operator or for the HWR-40 button, which has to finish before the Pi halts.
# Note this is a CEILING, not a delay: a normal stop completes as soon as the
# cgroup is empty, typically in ~2 s. The 20 s only ever elapses in the
# pathological case, and in that case you want the full escalation to run.
TimeoutStopSec=20

# ExecStop WIRED 2026-08-25. The interlock below was not bypassed -- both of its
# named conditions were cleared on the robot that day, and the evidence is here so
# nobody has to reconstruct it:
#
#   * Q-SD3 -- MEASURED. A full teardown through `gripperx-stack-stop --mode=stop`
#     took 14.043 s first-observed-exit to last, with ORPHANS: none. The whole ROS
#     stack was down in 1.449 s; the remainder is the Docker micro-ROS agent, a
#     different unit, so TimeoutStopSec=20 above is untouched and still ample.
#     One run only -- the shutdown track asks for three before the NUMBER is
#     fixed, and the number is not being changed here.
#   * D-SD1 -- DEPLOYED and VERIFIED TWICE, which is what this block demanded.
#     (a) Cold boot 11:33 (true power cycle, systemd-started bringup):
#         `No command written on activation (SR-14 item 1)`, steering HELD at the
#         limp angles [-0.0890, 0.0567, 0.0368, -0.0705] rad.
#     (b) Full restart via `gripperx-stack-stop --mode=restart --approve-motion`,
#         robot ON BLOCKS, explicit per-test user approval (SR-1), which is also
#         SR-12's owed restart test: /hw/steer_states BYTE-IDENTICAL before and
#         after -- [0.0046045118457136835, -0.0030696745638091223,
#         0.004601043722304911, 0.006139349127618245].
#
# ALSO CLEARED THE SAME DAY, and it is what makes this ExecStop mean something:
# phase 1's probe is `python3 -c 'import ros2controlcli'`, and under sudo and
# under ExecStop= the ROS environment is absent, so it reported `no-cli` and
# announced "an INSTALL defect on this machine ... HWR-40 crit. 6 NOT met". The
# package was installed all along (4.45.2); only the environment was missing.
# gripperx-stack-stop now sources it itself. Without that fix this ExecStop would
# have warned on EVERY stop and still fallen back to the ESP32 backstop -- the
# very mechanism HWR-40 criterion 6 forbids.
#
# --from-unit is mandatory here: this runs INSIDE the stop job of the unit that
# phase 3 would otherwise `systemctl stop`, which deadlocks until TimeoutStopSec
# expires. It runs PHASE 1 ONLY -- the ordered, commanded stop -- and touches no
# unit; systemd already owns the lifecycle in this context.
ExecStop=/usr/local/bin/gripperx-stack-stop --mode=stop --from-unit

# --- historical rationale, kept because it records WHY this was blocked --------
# NO ExecStop YET -- and this is a hard safety interlock, not an omission.
# The ordered sequence belongs here (SR-12 rev 3 / S2 entry point 2: ONE script,
# `gripperx-stack-stop --mode=...`, called by the operator CLI, by this
# ExecStop, by the launch-side handler and later by the HWR-40 GPIO button).
# It MUST NOT be wired up before all three of these are cleared:
#
#   * Q-SD3 -- the user's choice of the contract shape itself. THIS IS NOW THE
#     ONLY REMAINING BLOCKER.
#
# DEFECT D-SD1 -- FIXED 2026-08-19, user-authorised, and NOW DEPLOYED AND
# VERIFIED ON THE ROBOT (corrected 2026-08-25; this block used to say "NOT YET
# DEPLOYED"). GripperXInterface::on_deactivate() called publish_zero_commands(),
# which assigns 0.0 to all 8 joints over a steering-first layout, so every
# controller deactivation published steering 0 rad and steer_servo_node drove
# all four servos to centre -- an ordered ExecStop would have PERFORMED the
# movement this contract exists to prevent. It now calls publish_stop_commands()
# (gripperx_interface.cpp L170): wheels to zero, steering held. Together with the
# stale-command hold in steer_servo_node._on_timer, the ordered controller
# shutdown is motion-free in the source AND on the robot: measured 2026-08-25,
# a cold boot (systemd-started, true power cycle) held the measured/limp angles
# and wrote no command on activation, and a full restart under per-test approval
# with the robot on blocks left /hw/steer_states byte-identical before and after.
# TWO THINGS THAT DOES NOT CHANGE: (a) the result is conditional on
# center_on_startup: false and on the INTERIM arm_home_on_startup:=false (issue
# #340) -- see the Restart=on-failure note further down; (b) a restart is still a
# motion TRIGGER under SR-1 and still needs per-test approval. Wiring ExecStop is
# still gated on Q-SD3 above.
#
# DECIDED BY THE USER 2026-08-19 and therefore NO LONGER blockers: Q-SD1/Q-SD2.
# Option 0 -- no new firmware interface. The ESP
# teardown is Option 0 (stop the agent, let the ESP's ping timeout fire its own
# destroyEntities()), so no firmware interface is needed and the halt path has
# no ESP wait in it at all. The only ESP-derived timing left lives in the
# RESTART path, not here -- see the dwell in gripperx-stack-stop (renamed 2026-08-25 from
# .sh.proposal: it is deployed and serving a P0 chain, so a name saying "proposal"
# was misleading).
#
# Rejected outright, so it is not re-proposed: an ExecStop that merely publishes
# zero commands. It would make the stop path an extra writer on a command topic
# (SR-9), and it is redundant -- once the nodes are gone nothing publishes and
# the ESP32's own 1000 ms receive timeout zeroes the motors. Note this is the
# BACKSTOP, which HWR-40 criterion 6 explicitly forbids as the mechanism; the
# mechanism is the commanded stop in the sequence above, which is what is still
# missing. Today's contract makes the teardown clean and orphan-free; it does
# NOT yet satisfy HWR-40 criterion 6.

# Orphan check for the SR-12 (5) acceptance criterion: records what is still
# alive after the stop, so the journal answers "were there orphans?" without
# anyone having to be logged in at the time. Read-only, "-" prefixed, and it
# deliberately also reports processes from a MANUAL `ros2 launch` (which this
# unit does not own) -- that is information, not an action.
ExecStopPost=-/bin/sh -c 'pgrep -a -f "gripperx_|ros2_control_node|robot_state_publisher|steer_servo|arm_action_server|teleop_mux" || echo "[stop] no chain processes left"'

# Restart=on-failure is KEPT, but note what it means: an automatic restart is a
# motion trigger (SR-1/SR-12). It is only acceptable because the two startup
# moves are off -- arm_home_on_startup:=false in gripperx-bringup.sh (issue
# #340) and center_on_startup: false in steer_servo.yaml (OP-20 option B).
# If either is ever turned back on, this line has to be reconsidered.
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
